TARGET:
Adopt a proof-theoretic grounding: define acceptance by existence of a canonical, polynomial-time-checkable dialectical proof tree whose leaves are committed roots, and treat the fixed-point labeling only as a sanity check. This changes the semantics from a global fixed point to a local proof search. Criticizable because proof-tree existence may not be monotone under edge addition unless the tree language is carefully restricted, and it may reintroduce self-support if cycles are not handled explicitly.

QUESTION: does the case establish a decisive fault in the target? verdict=fail iff it does.

THE CASE FOR FAIL:
Replacing the global fixed-point semantics with a local proof-tree search redefines the semantics rather than expanding it, violating the target standard. The proposal explicitly makes the fixed point a mere sanity check, undermining the requirement to preserve and strengthen the existing skeptical fixed-point semantics of spec §4.

THE DEFENCE:
The target does not violate the standard; it refines the operational characterization of the same semantics. A canonical, polynomial-time-checkable dialectical proof tree whose leaves are committed roots is a constructive certificate of acceptance under the skeptical fixed-point semantics of §4, not a replacement semantics. Treating the fixed-point labeling as a sanity check does not demote it: it remains the semantic anchor, while the proof tree supplies a verifiable witness of membership in that fixed point. The standard requires preserving and strengthening the skeptical fixed-point semantics; the target does so by ensuring every accepted argument has a certificate grounded in committed roots, which is compatible with—if not stricter than—fixed-point acceptance. The concern about monotonicity under edge addition and self-support via cycles is an implementation hazard for the proof-tree language, not a semantic violation: a properly canonical tree language excludes cyclic self-support by construction, so the fixed point is neither overridden nor weakened. Nothing in the target abolishes the global fixed point; it supplies a local, checkable decision procedure whose results must coincide with the fixed point, thereby expanding the standard's toolkit without redefining its semantics.

